---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="Zakiel" lang={Astro.currentLocale || "en"}>
  <section class="font-fig">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tighter mb-4">Music App</h1>
    <div>
      <p class="text-lg">
        Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet consectetur adipisicing elit.
      </p>
      <div>Video Demo</div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10">
      <span class="font-bold tracking-tighter text-2xl">Why I Built This </span>
      <div class="mt-2 flex flex-col gap-4 font-medium">
        <p>
          I love music. It's one of my favorite things in life, and i listen to
          so many genres that I have over 6,000 songs saved on Spotify...
        </p>
        <span class="text-xl md:text-lg font-semibold italic leading-snug"
          >Well...</span
        >
        <p class="text-base">
          I realized there was no easy way to navigate my own library or find
          the right songs for a specific moment. I missed a tag-based system to
          search quickly by mood, genre or context.
        </p>
        <span class="text-xl md:text-lg font-semibold italic leading-snug"
          >So I decided to build my own music app.</span
        >
        <p>
          I wanted it to be fast, functional, and minimal — a player that could
          import local songs, organize them with tags, and search by mood or
          genre without sacrificing performance or UX.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-10">
      <span class="font-bold tracking-tighter text-2xl"
        >Tech I Used — and Why I Chose Them</span
      >
      <div class="list-disc list-inside mt-2 flex flex-col gap-4">
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-2 font-medium text-base mt-4">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold">🧱 HTML, CSS, Typescript</h3>

          <p class="mb-2">
            I chose modern web technologies to ensure scalability and
            maintainability beyond a simple prototype. My goal was to build a
            foundation that could evolve into a high-quality desktop and mobile
            music application.
          </p>
          <p class="mb-4">
            This decision allowed me to focus on creating the <span
              class="font-bold">music player I always wanted</span
            > — elegant, fast, and responsive.
          </p>

          <!-- when it was just a personal project -->
          <!-- But given the context
          that I wanted this app to be scalable to a high-quality desktop and  -->
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-2 font-medium text-base">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold">🦀 Rust + Tauri</h3>
          <p class="mb-2">
            At first, I considered Electron. However, after deeper research, I
            decided to use <span class="font-bold">Tauri</span>, which provides
            the same ability to build desktop applications with web
            technologies, but with much greater efficiency.
          </p>
          <p class="mb-2">
            Tauri relies on the system’s native WebView instead of bundling
            Chromium, resulting in smaller build sizes, lower memory
            consumption, and faster startup times.
          </p>
          <p class="mb-2">
            By using <span class="font-semibold">Rust</span> for the backend, I gained
            hands-on experience with systems-level programming. I learned about memory
            safety, concurrency, and efficient audio decoding, which directly improved
            the app's performance and reliability, while broadening my technical
            depth as a developer.
          </p>
          <p class="mb-4">
            Learning Rust was genuinely exciting — It challenged me to think
            differently about performance, safety, and system-level design.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-2 font-medium text-base">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold">⚛️ React + Tailwind</h3>
          <p class="mb-2">
            Since I’m very comfortable with both, <span class="font-semibold"
              >React</span
            > and
            <span class="font-semibold">Tailwind CSS</span> were the perfect combination
            to build the interface quickly and efficiently.
          </p>
          <p class="mb-4">
            This combination allowed me to rapidly prototype, test ideas, and
            achieving a polished design that reflects my personal design
            standards.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10">
      <span class="font-bold tracking-tight text-2xl"
        >Challenges & Learnings</span
      >
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-6 font-medium">
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-2 mt-6">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-lg">1. Learning Rust from Scratch</h3>
          <p class="mb-2">
            <span class="font-semibold italic">Challenge:</span> Although I already
            had experience with JavaScript and C#, Rust introduces new concepts like
            ownership, borrowing, lifetimes, and explicit memory management — all
            very different from what I was used to. It required a significant learning
            curve.
          </p>
          <p class="mb-2">
            <span class="font-semibold italic">How I approached it:</span> I studied
            the official Rust book, completed practical exercises, and reimplemented
            parts of the audio playback system to understand how memory safety and
            error handling work at a low level.
          </p>
          <p class="mb-4">
            <span class="font-semibold italic">What I learned / Result:</span>
            I developed confidence in writing low-level code and achieved a solid
            understanding of Rust. This allowed me to build efficient and safe backend
            systems that serve as the foundation of this music player.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-lg">2. Audio Playback</h3>
          <p class="mb-2">
            <span class="font-semibold italic">Challenge:</span> Designing a player
            capable of stable and low-latency audio playback, even when managing
            large local music libraries. Achieving a balance between performance,
            responsiveness, and reliability was the main goal.
          </p>
          <p class="mb-2">
            <span class="font-semibold italic">How I approached it:</span> I evaluated
            two approaches: implementing playback through the Web Audio API or delegating
            it entirely to a Rust backend. I ultimately chose Rust because it provided
            deeper control and better performance in the long run. Implementing a
            custom audio backend required low-level handling: decoding formats, sample
            conversion, efficient buffering, and synchronization strategies while
            keeping the React UI smooth and responsive.
          </p>
          <p class="mb-4">
            <span class="font-semibold italic">What I learned / Result:</span>
            I gained a solid understanding of low-level audio processing and input/output
            optimization. The final result was a fast, reliable, and responsive playback
            system that matched the performance and quality I envisioned for the
            application.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-lg">3. Core Player Features</h3>
          <p class="mb-2">
            <span class="font-semibold italic">Challenge:</span> Implementing essential
            features such as playlists, CRUD operations for songs, playback queues,
            thumbnails, and tag-based search—without compromising responsiveness
            as the music library size increased.
          </p>
          <p class="mb-2">
            <span class="font-semibold italic">How I approached it:</span> I implemented
            the logic for each feature, list virtualization, lazy thumbnail loading,
            asynchronous I/O, and an efficient tag-based search engine. My approach
            was to ensure solid core logic first, then refine the design for a polished,
            intuitive user experience.
          </p>
          <p class="mb-4">
            <span class="font-semibold italic">What I learned / Result:</span>
            The player maintains smooth performance even with extensive libraries.
            Core features such as playlist management, tag editing, and real-time
            search perform consistently and efficiently, achieving the balance between
            technical robustness and user experience I aimed for.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- <div class="mt-10">
        <span class="font-bold tracking-tighter text-2xl">Roadmap / Future</span
        >
        <div class="mt-2 flex flex-col gap-4 font-medium"></div>
      </div> -->
      <div class="mt-10">
        <span class="font-bold tracking-tighter text-2xl"
          >App Demo & Screenshots</span
        >
        <p>Tip: click the image to make it full screen</p>
        <div class="mt-2 flex flex-col gap-4 font-medium"></div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-10">
        <span class="font-bold tracking-tighter text-2xl">Try it out!</span>
        <p class="text-base mt-2 font-medium">
          The app is still in state prototype, but if you want to check it out,
          you can download the lastest build.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
